// `truncate_chars` became `truncate` and `reverse_string` became `reverse` on 2026-10-23, and
// `mutable_string_get` became `char_at`'s builder member the same day. All three old names point
// home.
//
// **Two of these are suffixes, which the shared message had to grow to cover.** It said "these
// names lost the prefix that said which world the first argument came from" -- true of every entry
// in that table until `_chars` and `_string` arrived. The rule is the same read from either end: a
// word naming the type of argument one, which argument one says. The second time that sentence has
// been widened rather than left half-true, after "a builder is a mutable collection" went.
//
// **`_chars` was made redundant by a different change.** It said which unit the limit counts in,
// back when `len` counted bytes and the distinction had to be in the name. `len` counts characters
// as of the same week, so characters are what the prelude means by a length unless it says
// `byte_len`. The parameter keeps `max_chars`, which is where the unit now lives -- at the call
// site, where it is read.
//
// **`char_at` is the one of the three that cost C.** The other prefix leftovers derived the symbol
// already registered; `char_at` was registered *bare*, which `native_lookup(fn->name)` finds
// before the world-prefix fallback is consulted -- so both members would have run the `string`
// implementation and read a builder handle as text. That is the trap `last_index_of` fell into the
// day before. `string_char_at` and `mutable_string_char_at` now.
fn main() {
    // truncate, with the unit in the parameter rather than the name
    print(truncate("héllo world", 5))
    print(truncate("héllo", 99))

    // char_at over both worlds, which is what the merge bought
    val s = "héllo"
    val sb = mutable_string_new()
    append(sb, "héllo")
    print(char_at(s, 0))
    print(char_at(sb, 0))
    print(char_at(sb, 1) == char_at(s, 1))

    // reverse over all three members of its set, code points not bytes
    print(reverse("héllo"))
    print(reverse([1, 2, 3]))
    val b = mutable_array_new<int>()
    push(b, 1, 2, 3)
    reverse(b)
    print(snapshot(b))
}
